BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (10. A harsh systolic murmur develops during blood ejection through a narrowed aortic valve. What mechanism) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 731.3 Tm (explains it?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 714.6 Tm (Answer: B. Turbulent forward flow through a stenotic outflow orifice.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.3 Tm (Explanation: Aortic stenosis accelerates blood through a narrow outlet during systole, causing a turbulent ejection) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 685.2 Tm (murmur.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 669.1 Tm (Not A: The aortic valve opens when ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure in systole.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 655.7 Tm (Not C: Mitral regurgitation usually occurs during systole, not diastole.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 642.3 Tm (Not D: Laminar flow through normal valves ordinarily produces no such murmur.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 628.9 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q32 | 31-33; Chapter 5 ?7) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 605.7 Tm (11. At 25 mm/s, a PR interval extends across 4.5 small squares. Which interpretation is most accurate?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 589.0 Tm (Answer: D. 0.18 s, inside the stated PR-interval range.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 572.7 Tm (Explanation: The horizontal duration is 4.5 x 0.04 = 0.18 s. This lies within the 0.12-0.20 s interval given in the) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 559.6 Tm (lecture.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 543.5 Tm (Not A: This is not the product of 4.5 and 0.04 s.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 530.1 Tm (Not B: This incorrectly halves the duration obtained from 4.5 small squares.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 516.7 Tm (Not C: This incorrectly assigns 0.20 s to each small square.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 503.3 Tm (Source: lecture-7:Q37 | 7, 13) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 480.1 Tm (12. Why does fetal haemoglobin load oxygen effectively at the placental interface?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 463.4 Tm (Answer: B. Its weaker 2,3-BPG binding increases oxygen affinity.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 447.1 Tm (Explanation: HbF interacts less with 2,3-BPG, shifting its oxygen dissociation curve left and favouring oxygen) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 434.0 Tm (uptake from maternal blood.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 417.9 Tm (Not A: Stronger 2,3-BPG binding would lower oxygen affinity.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 404.5 Tm (Not C: Fetal haemoglobin still contains iron-bearing haem groups.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 391.1 Tm (Not D: Oxygen binding is reversible, not permanent.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 377.7 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q58 | 45) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 354.5 Tm (13. During severe hepatic hypoperfusion, cells near the central vein are especially vulnerable to hypoxia.) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 340.8 Tm (Why?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 324.1 Tm (Answer: C. They are farthest downstream from the portal triads.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 307.8 Tm (Explanation: Centrilobular cells receive blood after it has travelled from portal triads, making them less) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 294.7 Tm (oxygenated and vulnerable.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 278.6 Tm (Not A: Centrilobular hepatocytes remain within the sinusoidal perfusion system.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 265.2 Tm (Not B: Oxygenated hepatic arterial inflow enters near the portal triads.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 251.8 Tm (Not D: Blood oxygen content generally falls as it traverses sinusoids.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 238.4 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q41 | 35) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 215.2 Tm (14. Following a substantial meal, intestinal blood flow rises for several hours. What primarily drives this) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 201.5 Tm (response?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 184.8 Tm (Answer: D. Local metabolic activity and absorptive demand.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 168.5 Tm (Explanation: Postprandial metabolic and absorptive activity promotes local gastrointestinal hyperaemia; the) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 155.4 Tm (lecture notes it can last about three hours.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 139.3 Tm (Not A: Sympathetic constriction usually opposes the flow increase.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 125.9 Tm (Not B: Absorption increases rather than abolishes local work.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 112.5 Tm (Not C: Portal flow continues to deliver absorbed substances to liver.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 99.1 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q42 | 36) Tj ET
