BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (15. Two Frank-Starling curves are plotted against identical end-diastolic volumes. What does an upward shift) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 731.3 Tm (of the entire curve usually indicate?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 714.6 Tm (Answer: A. Greater contractility at the same preload.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.3 Tm (Explanation: Positive inotropy raises stroke volume or output at the same filling level, shifting the) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 685.2 Tm (Frank-Starling function upward.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 669.1 Tm (Not B: Higher afterload tends to impair ejection.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 655.7 Tm (Not C: Valve closure timing is not the definition of an upward Starling curve.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 642.3 Tm (Not D: Lower force would shift the curve downward, not upward.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 628.9 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q37 | 39-42) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 605.7 Tm (16. During a stress response, circulating adrenaline dilates vessels in working skeletal muscle while) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 592.0 Tm (constricting many skin vessels. Why?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 575.3 Tm (Answer: B. Different vascular beds express different adrenergic receptors.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 559.0 Tm (Explanation: beta2 effects can favour dilation in skeletal muscle, while alpha1 effects cause constriction in many) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 545.9 Tm (skin and gut vessels.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 529.8 Tm (Not A: Muscle vessels respond because they possess adrenergic receptors.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 516.4 Tm (Not C: The difference is due mainly to receptor distribution rather than selective circulating delivery.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 503.0 Tm (Not D: Plasma volume differences do not explain opposite receptor responses.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 489.6 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q32 | 12-13, 30-32) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 466.4 Tm (17. In severe haemorrhagic shock, why does sympathetic constriction of splanchnic vessels help initially?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 449.7 Tm (Answer: D. It redistributes flow and mobilises stored venous blood.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 433.4 Tm (Explanation: The splanchnic bed is a large blood reservoir; vasoconstriction redirects perfusion and recruits) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 420.3 Tm (stored blood toward central circulation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 404.2 Tm (Not A: Mucosal flow may decrease, risking ischaemia.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 390.8 Tm (Not B: Extraction is not necessarily complete.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 377.4 Tm (Not C: Severe or prolonged constriction can contribute to intestinal injury.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 364.0 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q43 | 36) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 340.8 Tm (18. An examination reveals raised JVP, a tender enlarged liver, ascites and ankle oedema. Which haemodynamic) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 327.1 Tm (change unifies these?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 310.4 Tm (Answer: A. High systemic venous pressure behind the right ventricle.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 294.1 Tm (Explanation: Right ventricular backward failure elevates right atrial and systemic venous pressures, causing) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 281.0 Tm (jugular distension, hepatic congestion and dependent oedema.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 264.9 Tm (Not B: Higher systemic venous pressure promotes, rather than reduces, filtration.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 251.5 Tm (Not C: Isolated pulmonary venous congestion principally reflects left-sided failure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 238.1 Tm (Not D: Right-sided backward failure increases rather than decreases venous pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 224.7 Tm (Source: circulation-3:Q56 | Circulation 3, slides 33) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 201.5 Tm (19. Immediately after the first effective breaths, what happens to pulmonary vascular resistance?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 184.8 Tm (Answer: D. It falls as the lungs expand and oxygen tension rises.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 168.5 Tm (Explanation: Lung aeration and increased alveolar oxygen rapidly lower pulmonary vascular resistance, increasing) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 155.4 Tm (pulmonary blood flow.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 139.3 Tm (Not A: Alveolar oxygen generally relieves pulmonary hypoxic vasoconstriction.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 125.9 Tm (Not B: Pulmonary adaptation begins with the first breaths.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 112.5 Tm (Not C: Pulmonary and systemic circuits retain very different resistance values.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 99.1 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q59 | 45; Chapter 27 ?4) Tj ET
