BT /F2 12.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 815.0 Tm (DOMiNANCE / CVS PHYSIOLOGY) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 798.0 Tm (MOCK 04 | 40Q | EXPLANATION GUIDE | 60 MIN) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (25. What conservation principle underlies Fick measurement of cardiac output?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 728.3 Tm (Answer: C. Oxygen use equals flow x arterial-venous oxygen difference.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 712.0 Tm (Explanation: By mass balance, oxygen uptake across lungs and consumption by tissues equal cardiac output multiplied) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.9 Tm (by arterial-venous oxygen content difference.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 682.8 Tm (Not A: Tissues extract oxygen, so venous content is generally lower.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 669.4 Tm (Not B: Extraction and flow together determine total oxygen consumption.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 656.0 Tm (Not D: The body consumes oxygen but does not produce it.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 642.6 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q45 | 52-53) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 619.4 Tm (26. What keeps tissue fluid moving through collecting lymph vessels toward the great veins?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 602.7 Tm (Answer: A. Valves, muscle movement and lymphatic contractions.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 586.4 Tm (Explanation: One-way valves, skeletal-muscle compression, respiratory movement and lymphatic smooth-muscle) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 573.3 Tm (contraction promote forward flow. The valves prevent reflux between compressions, making the low-pressure pumping) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 560.2 Tm (effective.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 544.1 Tm (Not B: Whole vessels do not diffuse through tissues.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 530.7 Tm (Not C: Backflow is limited by valves rather than required for transport.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 517.3 Tm (Not D: Lymph flow is not driven directly by systemic arterial pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 503.9 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q94 | 60; Chapter 15 ?3) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 480.7 Tm (27. A patient with left-sided heart failure feels breathless when lying flat but improves after sitting up.) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 467.0 Tm (Why?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 450.3 Tm (Answer: A. Lying flat shifts peripheral blood toward congested lungs.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 434.0 Tm (Explanation: Supine positioning returns peripheral blood to the thorax; a failing left ventricle cannot handle the) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 420.9 Tm (extra flow, increasing pulmonary venous congestion.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 404.8 Tm (Not B: Central thoracic volume commonly increases rather than decreases supine.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 391.4 Tm (Not C: A higher pulmonary capillary pressure would worsen breathlessness.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 378.0 Tm (Not D: Sitting upright tends to reduce thoracic venous return.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 364.6 Tm (Source: circulation-3:Q55 | Circulation 3, slides 33; Chapter 21 ?3) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 341.4 Tm (28. A regular rhythm has 24 small squares between adjacent R waves at standard paper speed. Approximately) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 327.7 Tm (what is the heart rate?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 311.0 Tm (Answer: C. 63 beats/min.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 294.7 Tm (Explanation: The rate in a regular rhythm is 1500 divided by the R-R separation in small squares: 1500/24 = 62.5) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 281.6 Tm (beats/min, approximately 63.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 265.5 Tm (Not A: 50 beats/min corresponds to about 30 small squares between R waves.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 252.1 Tm (Not B: 80 beats/min corresponds to about 18.75 small squares between R waves.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 238.7 Tm (Not D: 60 beats/min corresponds to about 25 small squares between R waves.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 225.3 Tm (Source: lecture-7:Q41 | 13-14) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 202.1 Tm (29. What is detected by the fingers when palpating the radial pulse?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 185.4 Tm (Answer: B. An arterial wall-distension pressure wave.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 169.1 Tm (Explanation: Each ventricular ejection creates an arterial pressure/distension wave that travels along the) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 156.0 Tm (arteries. This pressure wave moves faster than the bulk blood itself.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 139.9 Tm (Not A: Venous blood does not directly pulse into systemic arteries.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 126.5 Tm (Not C: Red cells are not separately felt during palpation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 113.1 Tm (Not D: The palpable pulse is mechanical rather than an electrical conduction wave.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 99.7 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q66 | 22-23) Tj ET
