q .08 .14 .26 rg 0 792 595 50 re f Q
BT /F2 12.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 815.0 Tm (DOMiNANCE / CVS PHYSIOLOGY) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 798.0 Tm (MOCK 04 | 40Q | EXPLANATION GUIDE | 60 MIN) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (30. V2 has been placed correctly and V4 is at its usual site. Where should V3 be attached?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 728.3 Tm (Answer: D. Halfway between the V2 and V4 electrodes.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 712.0 Tm (Explanation: V3 is placed between the already positioned V2 and V4, so the anatomical line between those contacts) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.9 Tm (determines its location.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 682.8 Tm (Not A: The midaxillary line at V4 level is used for V6.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 669.4 Tm (Not B: The anterior axillary line at V4 level is used for V5.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 656.0 Tm (Not C: The fourth right parasternal position belongs to V1.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 642.6 Tm (Source: lecture-7:Q12 | 5; Chapter 6 ?3) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 619.4 Tm (31. Where is the largest controllable fall in systemic vascular pressure normally produced?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 602.7 Tm (Answer: C. Across resistance arterioles.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 586.4 Tm (Explanation: Small arteries and arterioles provide substantial resistance, producing a major pressure drop from) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 573.3 Tm (arterial to capillary levels.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 557.2 Tm (Not A: Large veins carry blood at low pressure and resistance.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 543.8 Tm (Not B: Right atrial pressure is low but does not create the main distributed arterial drop.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 530.4 Tm (Not D: Large elastic arteries conduct flow with relatively small resistance.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 517.0 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q65 | 57, 61, 76; Chapter 10 ?9) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 493.8 Tm (32. Why does the sinoatrial node ordinarily set the heart's rhythm rather than latent pacemakers?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 477.1 Tm (Answer: B. It has the fastest intrinsic depolarisation rate.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 460.8 Tm (Explanation: The SA node normally reaches threshold fastest and overdrive-suppresses slower latent pacemakers.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 447.7 Tm (Faster SA-node impulses normally suppress slower escape pacemakers.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 431.6 Tm (Not A: Action potential duration is not the primary reason for the fastest spontaneous rate.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 418.2 Tm (Not C: Coronary perfusion is not determined by pulmonary venous timing.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 404.8 Tm (Not D: Pacemaker dominance does not depend on muscle mass.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 391.4 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q12 | 7-9; Chapter 3 ?3) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 368.2 Tm (33. Why can coordinated cardiac pumping continue without a new external nerve impulse for every beat?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 351.5 Tm (Answer: B. Intrinsic pacemaker cells generate rhythmic impulses.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 335.2 Tm (Explanation: Specialised cardiac pacemaker cells depolarise spontaneously; autonomic nerves modulate rather than) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 322.1 Tm (initiate each heartbeat.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 306.0 Tm (Not A: Atrial conduction is not limited to expiration.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 292.6 Tm (Not C: The SA node produces electrical impulses, not direct ejection pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 279.2 Tm (Not D: Some conducting cells possess spontaneous automaticity.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 265.8 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q4 | 7-10) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 242.6 Tm (34. A patient has near-normal resting cardiac output but very high ventricular filling pressures and) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 228.9 Tm (pulmonary congestion. Which statement fits best?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 212.2 Tm (Answer: A. Heart failure can occur with normal output.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 195.9 Tm (Explanation: Heart failure includes states where output is maintained only at abnormally high filling pressures,) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 182.8 Tm (causing congestion.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 166.7 Tm (Not B: High filling pressures can be evidence of dysfunction rather than normality.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 153.3 Tm (Not C: Diastolic failure can occur with preserved ejection fraction.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 139.9 Tm (Not D: A near-normal output can be maintained at harmful filling pressures.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 126.5 Tm (Source: circulation-3:Q47 | Circulation 3, slide 31; Chapter 21 ?1) Tj ET
