BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (35. Which pressure difference primarily drives blood back to the right atrium from the body?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 728.3 Tm (Answer: C. Systemic filling pressure minus right atrial pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 712.0 Tm (Explanation: Venous return depends on the upstream systemic filling pressure relative to right atrial pressure and) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.9 Tm (the resistance to venous return.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 682.8 Tm (Not A: Aortic-right ventricular systolic pressure is not the venous return driving gradient.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 669.4 Tm (Not B: That difference is a pulse-pressure-related comparison.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 656.0 Tm (Not D: Pulmonary venous pressure does not define systemic venous return.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 642.6 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q74 | 97; Chapter 12 ?3) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 619.4 Tm (36. What can follow when the right ventricle fails to deliver adequate forward flow to the lungs?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 602.7 Tm (Answer: B. Less blood reaches the left heart for systemic output.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 586.4 Tm (Explanation: Reduced right ventricular output limits pulmonary flow and subsequent return to the left atrium,) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 573.3 Tm (compromising left-sided filling and delivery.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 557.2 Tm (Not A: The right heart cannot deliver excess flow when severely failing.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 543.8 Tm (Not C: Reduced pulmonary transit cannot raise systemic oxygen delivery.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 530.4 Tm (Not D: Less pulmonary venous return tends to decrease left-sided filling.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 517.0 Tm (Source: circulation-3:Q54 | Circulation 3, slides 32) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 493.8 Tm (37. At an otherwise unchanged capillary, how would increased interstitial protein concentration affect fluid) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 480.1 Tm (movement?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 463.4 Tm (Answer: D. It strengthens the outward oncotic force favouring filtration.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 447.1 Tm (Explanation: More interstitial protein increases interstitial colloid osmotic pressure, tending to pull fluid) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 434.0 Tm (outward from the capillary and increase filtration.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 417.9 Tm (Not A: Increased proteins outside the capillary attract rather than remove interstitial water.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 404.5 Tm (Not B: Plasma protein concentration is held constant; the changed force is interstitial.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 391.1 Tm (Not C: A change in interstitial protein does not directly lower capillary pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 377.7 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q83 | 85-88) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 354.5 Tm (38. Electrical stimulation of the medullary pressor area increases arterial pressure. What is the principal) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 340.8 Tm (neural pathway?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 324.1 Tm (Answer: D. Excitation of sympathetic neurons in the spinal cord.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 307.8 Tm (Explanation: The pressor area sends excitatory fibres down to the sympathetic neurons in the spinal cord. More) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 294.7 Tm (sympathetic output constricts arterioles and veins and speeds and strengthens the heart, so pressure rises.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 278.6 Tm (Not A: Carotid sinus fibres carry sensory information towards the medulla.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 265.2 Tm (Not B: Inhibiting sympathetic neurons would tend to lower pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 251.8 Tm (Not C: Pressor-area activity does not require direct pacemaker inhibition.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 238.4 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q22 | 16, 19, 21) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 215.2 Tm (39. A patient has low circulating volume. Which hormone chiefly increases renal water retention and can) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 201.5 Tm (constrict vessels at high concentration?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 184.8 Tm (Answer: A. Vasopressin, also called ADH.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 168.5 Tm (Explanation: ADH promotes renal water conservation and has a vasoconstrictor action when its level is high.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 152.4 Tm (Not B: ANP promotes sodium and water loss.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 139.0 Tm (Not C: VIP is a vasodilator rather than a water-retaining hormone.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 125.6 Tm (Not D: Prostacyclin dilates vessels and limits platelet aggregation.) Tj ET
